<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Delhi Sultanate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Delhi Sultanate was a series of Muslim dynasties that ruled much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> between the early thirteenth and early sixteenth centuries. It repelled Mongol incursions from the Chagatai realm, suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-delhi-1398">sack of Delhi</a> in 1398, and ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> sultan at Panipat in 1526, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate.jpg" alt="Delhi Sultanate" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maps created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan; Territorial area: पाटलिपुत्र, per Schwartberg Atlas p.147 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Mamluk_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The sultanate was established in 1206 in the former Ghurid territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its foundation was laid by the Ghurid conqueror Muhammad Ghori, who defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> confederacy led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a> near Tarain in 1192. It began as one of several principalities held by Muhammad Ghori's Turkic slave-generals, who divided the Ghurid lands among themselves.</p><p>The rise of the sultanate belonged to a wider movement of Turkic nomads from the Central Asian steppes into Muslim lands. Muslim rulers enslaved Turks and raised many of them as army slaves, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and some became rulers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</a>, a former slave of Muhammad Ghori, was the first sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history is usually divided into five periods: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> (1206 to 1290), Khalji (1290 to 1320), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> (1320 to 1414), Sayyid (1414 to 1451) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> (1451 to 1526). Under the Khalji and Tughlaq dynasties conquests continued deep into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the sultanate reached its largest extent under the Tughlaq Muhammad bin Tughluq, when it held most of the subcontinent. Razia Sultana reigned from 1236 to 1240. The sultanate repelled attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s devastating raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1398 changed northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and the Kingdom of Mewar re-emerged, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and Bahmani sultanates asserted independence. In 1526 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, a Timurid ruler, invaded northern India and conquered the sultanate, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> succeeded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Chroniclers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziauddin-barani">Ziauddin Barani</a> called the state the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> called it Hind and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>. Its early elite consisted largely of first-generation immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The sultans generally accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> officials and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in the administration, and non-Muslim subjects paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> replaced private markets with government-run markets and imposed strict price controls.</p><p>The campaigns brought destruction. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-eaton">Richard Eaton</a> tabulated 37 instances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples desecrated or destroyed between 1234 and 1518, and notes that sultans also ordered temples protected or repaired. Sources record the sack of cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and large killings, among them 30,000 at Chittor under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> and 180,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> under Firuz Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a>.</p><p>The Hindustani language began to emerge in this period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a> wrote in a form of it he called Hindavi. Mosques and tombs under high domes and arches introduced Central Asian styles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Delhi Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bahmani Sultanate was born from a revolt against the <b>Delhi Sultanate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Muslim rule in Bengal began with the conquests of Bakhtiyar Khalji around 1204, and for more than a century the province was governed from Lakhnauti by governors of the <b>Delhi Sultanate</b> who often rebelled against their distant overlords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chagataid armies also crossed the Hindu Kush to invade the <b>Delhi Sultanate</b> repeatedly from the 1290s, under commanders sent by Duwa such as his son Qutlugh Khwaja, who led a great army to the outskirts of Delhi in 1299.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By annexing the Punjab and making Lahore a centre of Persian culture, where the Sufi Ali Hujwiri wrote the Kashf al-mahjub, the Ghaznavids prepared the ground for the Ghurids, the <b>Delhi Sultanate</b> and centuries of Persianate Muslim rule in India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mu'izz al-Din's victories at Tarain and Chandawar broke the leading Rajput and Gahadavala kingdoms of the Ganges plain, and his commanders created the military and administrative framework from which the <b>Delhi Sultanate</b> emerged in 1206.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gurjara-Pratiharas built the largest empire of northern India between Harsha and the <b>Delhi Sultanate</b>, held Arab power in check in Sindh and Multan for generations, and made Kannauj the prize of the tripartite struggle, while their feudatories became the Rajput dynasties that ruled the north after them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
